{%- comment -%}
  The card GRID for sections/dev-blog.liquid.

  Extracted because that section now renders in two places and they cannot share a loop: the blog
  index wraps its articles in a paginate block (a tag, so it has to bracket the loop in the section
  file), while the Our Story strip takes a plain `limit` and has nothing to paginate. Duplicating
  the loop would have duplicated the `sizes`/`widths` tables below it — the exact tables the long
  comment in dev-blog.liquid derives — and those are the first thing that would drift.

  The card itself is still snippets/dev-journal-cards-card.liquid. Nothing is re-implemented here;
  this only decides WHICH articles and with WHAT responsive-image budget.

  @param {object} articles      - an article collection (paginated slice, or blog.articles)
  @param {number} card_limit    - how many to render; always passed explicitly, never nil.
                                  Named `card_limit`, not `limit`, so the for-tag's own `limit:`
                                  keyword and the variable feeding it are never the same word.
  @param {number} card_level    - heading level for each card's title (2 on the index, 3 elsewhere)
  @param {string} cta_label     - "Read Article"
  @param {string} read_time_label - "min read"
  @param {string} author_prefix - "By"
  @param {boolean} [eager_first] - true only where the first card can really be the LCP element,
                                   i.e. the blog index. On Our Story this strip is the LAST section
                                   on a long page, so every card stays lazy.
{%- endcomment -%}

<ul class="dev-blog__grid journal-cards__list" role="list">
  {%- for article in articles limit: card_limit -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Same reading-time snippet as the Journal strip and the article page: metafield first,
      word count as the fallback. Three surfaces, one number.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture read_minutes -%}{%- render 'dev-article-read-time', article: article -%}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture article_byline -%}{%- render 'dev-article-author', article: article -%}{%- endcapture -%}

    {%- liquid
      assign card_meta = read_minutes | append: ' ' | append: read_time_label
      if article_byline != blank
        assign card_meta = card_meta | append: ' ㆍ' | append: author_prefix | append: ' ' | append: article_byline
      endif

      assign card_badge = article.tags | first
      assign card_alt = article.image.alt | default: article.title | escape

      assign card_eager = false
      if eager_first and forloop.first
        assign card_eager = true
      endif
    -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The `sizes` expression is the grid above, read back as arithmetic — 33vw was never
      true here and was wrong in BOTH directions. Column counts come from the auto-fill
      rule: a track is at least 320px, so three fit once the inner box reaches 1024
      (viewport 1152), two once it reaches 672 (viewport 800), one below that.

        viewport   layout          real card   33vw said
        1440       3 up            412px       475px   over  by 15%
        1024       2 up            424px       338px   UNDER by 20%
         800       1 up            672px       264px   UNDER by 60%

      Over-stating wastes bytes; under-stating ships a blurred card, which is worse and is
      why this is not just a byte fix. `vw` counts the scrollbar, so each branch is a few
      px generous — that is the safe direction.

      TWO desktop bands, split at 1300, because one ratio for the whole range is set by its
      narrowest card and then carries that crop all the way up. The floor is 1.4545 and it
      happens at exactly two viewports — 800, where two columns hit the 320px track minimum,
      and 1152, where three do. Everywhere else the card is wider:

        viewport   layout      card        box ratio
         800       2 up        320px       1.45   <- floor
        1152       3 up        320px       1.45   <- floor
        1300       3 up        369px       1.68
        1440       3 up        412px       1.87
        1920       3 up        576px       2.62

      So ≤1299 keeps 1.45, 1300–1699 gets 1.68, and ≥1700 gets 2.28 — each its own floor.
      Three bands rather than two because a band priced at its floor over-delivers at its
      ceiling, and with only two the 1.68 crop was still being carried out to 1920, where
      the box is 2.6: 1160x690 delivered for a 1145x440 box, 59% over.

      The split points are not round numbers for their own sake. 1300 is where the third
      column clears the 320px track minimum; 1700 is simply where the remaining overshoot
      stopped being worth another band. Above 2000 it climbs again and is left alone —
      there is no ratio that serves a fixed-height, fluid-width box at every width, only
      bands that bound the error.

      These numbers are properties of the GRID, not of the page the grid sits on, so they are
      identical in both placements: .dev-blog__grid is the same auto-fill rule with the same
      64px section padding and 32px gaps on Our Story as on /blogs/news.

      Only the FIRST card is eager, and only where `eager_first` says so. On the blog index it
      sits about 300px down on mobile — inside the viewport, so it is the likely LCP element, and
      a lazy LCP image is a guaranteed Lighthouse finding. The rest stay lazy: a 9-card index
      would otherwise open with nine parallel image requests.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- render 'dev-journal-cards-card',
      image: article.image,
      image_alt: card_alt,
      badge: card_badge,
      meta: card_meta,
      heading: article.title | escape,
      heading_level: card_level,
      url: article.url,
      cta_label: cta_label,
      eager: card_eager,
      tablet_max: 1299,
      ratio_tablet: 1.45,
      widths_tablet: '320, 400, 480, 560, 650, 740, 820, 900, 1000, 1100, 1200, 1300, 1360',
      sizes_tablet: '(min-width: 1152px) calc((100vw - 192px) / 3), (min-width: 800px) calc((100vw - 160px) / 2), calc(100vw - 128px)',
      ratio_desktop: 1.68,
      widths_desktop: '380, 460, 540, 620, 700, 750, 790, 840, 900, 960, 1060, 1160, 1300, 1450, 1600',
      sizes_desktop: 'calc((100vw - 192px) / 3)',
      wide_min: 1700,
      ratio_wide: 2.28,
      widths_wide: '500, 560, 620, 700, 780, 860, 950, 1020, 1100, 1160, 1250, 1350, 1500, 1600',
      sizes_wide: 'calc((100vw - 192px) / 3)'
    -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
</ul>
